      "section_type": "content",
      "id": 11,
      "title": "Geometry of Right Triangles",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "Continuing our exploration of geometry, we move from circles to triangles, specifically the right-angled triangle. A right triangle is any triangle that has one angle measuring exactly 90 degrees. This 90-degree angle is often marked with a small square. The sides of a right triangle have special names. The two sides that form the right angle are called the 'legs'. We can label them 'a' and 'b'. The third side, which is always the longest side and is opposite the right angle, is called the 'hypotenuse', which we'll label 'c'. There is a remarkable and ancient relationship between the lengths of these three sides, discovered by the Greek mathematician Pythagoras. This relationship is known as the Pythagorean theorem. It states that the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the two legs. As a formula, this is written a² + b² = c². This means if you draw a square on side 'a' and a square on side 'b', the combined area of those two squares will be exactly equal to the area of a square drawn on the hypotenuse 'c'.",
